  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluating the Convergence and Value of the Improper Integral</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the integral below and determine if it converges or diverges. 
</p>
<p>Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>.</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_lm@}}^{{@upper_lm@}}{@fx@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>Recall that we can’t evaluate the integral as long as there is an infinity in one of the limits. Therefore, we’ll need to replace infinity with a parameter \(t\) as follows,
</p>
<p>The given improper integral is</p>

<p>
\(\displaystyle  I = \int_{@lower_lm@}^{{@upper_lm@}}{@fx@} \, dx. \)
</p>

<p>We will first evaluate whether the integral converges or diverges by finding the limit of the integral as \( t \to \infty \):</p>

<p>
\( \displaystyle I =  \int_{@lower_lm@}^{{@b@}}{@fx@} \, dx. \)
</p>

<p>The antiderivative of \(\displaystyle {@fx@}  \) is:</p>

<p>
\(\displaystyle  \int {@fx@} \, dx = {@indef_int@} \)
</p>

<p>Now, evaluate the integral from \( {@lower_lm@} \) to \( t\):</p>

<p>
\(\displaystyle  I(t) = \left[  {@indef_int@} \right]_ {@lower_lm@}^t = {@indef_val@}\)
</p>

<p>Take the limit as \( t \to \infty \)</p>

<p>
\(\displaystyle  I = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left( {@indef_val@}\right) = {@def_integral@} \)
</p>

<p>The improper integral is {@mcq_correct(ta_4)[1]@}, and its exact value is \( \boxed{{@def_integral@}} \).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
